r even d7 if lines open. Crucially, Qg4-e6+ would be a devastating check if not for the pawn on e7 blocking the diagonal — but that pawn is actually mine (PWF1 on f6 suggests a recent capture). Wait — PWF1 on f6 indicates a white pawn on f6, meaning we have advanced into Black’s territory. That pawn could have come from e5xf6, opening the e-file.\n\nBut more importantly: can I deliver a crushing blow? Let’s look at **Qg4-e6** — is it check? From g4 to e6: yes, diagonal (same color square), and if nothing blocks, it attacks f7. Is the path clear? g4 → e6 passes through f5? No, queen moves diagonally: g4 to e6 is a diagonal move (g4-f5-e6), so f5 must be empty. Looking at f5: it's empty. e6 is empty. So Qg4-e6 is legal. And since the black king is on f7, e6 attacks f7 directly — so **Qe6+** is a check.\n\nAnd after Qe6+, the king cannot capture (no piece on e6), and where can it go? f7 to f8? But then Qxe7# if e7 is undefended. Wait — what’s on e7? In row 7: ...PBF0|KBF1|PBF0... — that means g7 has a black pawn, f7 is the king, e7 is a black pawn. So after Qe6+, King on f7 is attacked. Can it move?\n\n- f7-f8? Possible, but then Qxe7 is checkmate? Queen on e6 takes on e7: is that mate? After Kf8, Qxe7+?? But Qe7 is not check — the king is on f8, e7 is behind. Actually, Qe6 to e7 is vertical, not diagonal. Is Qe6-e7 allowed? Yes, queen can move vertically. But after Kf8, Qe7+ would be check, and if no one defends, it's mate. But does Black have defense?\n\nWait — let's clarify:\n\nAfter **Qg4-e6+**, Black king is in check on f7.\n\nPossible escapes:\n\n- f7-f8\n- f7-g7\n- f7-e7\n\nNow, is e7 defended? e7 has a black pawn (PBF0), so king can't capture its own pawn. So Ke7 is illegal. Kg7: g7 has a black pawn (PBF0), so also blocked. Only escape: Kf8.\n\nSo only legal move is Kf8.\n\nThen **Qe6xe7#** — queen captures on e7. Is that checkmate?\n\nQueen on e7, king on f8. Is the king in check? Yes — queen on e7 attacks f8 diagonally (e7-f8). Is there any defender? Rook on h8? No, h8 is empty. Bishop on f8? No. Any piece that can block or capture? Black has rook on a8, but can't reach e7. Knight on b8? But it's undeveloped. So no piece can stop Qxe7#. So **Qe6+ Kf8 Qxe7#** is mate in two.\n\nBut wait — is Qe6+ actually possible? What about interposition? Can Black block the check? The queen moves from g4 to e6 — diagonal. The squares between are f5. Is f5 occupied? No — f5 is empty. So no blocking possible. So Qe6+ is an unavoidable check, leading to mate.\n\nBut is Qg4-e6 in the valid move list?\n\nLooking at the valid moves:  \n**Q: g4-e6** — YES! It is listed.\n\nTherefore, this is a forced checkmate sequence.\n\nEvaluation:\n- Qg4-e6+: forces Kf8 (only move), then Qxe7#.\n- Immediate tactical knockout.\n- Other moves like Nf3 or pawn pushes are irrelevant compared to delivering mate.\n\nNo other move comes close in evaluation. This is a free win.\n\nReason: Qg4-e6 delivers a check that forces the black king to f8, after which Qxe7 is checkmate with no legal defense. This is the highest-value move possible — a forced checkmate in two.\n\nAnswer: Q: g4-e6"}
