\n1 |RWF0|    |BWF0|    |KWF0|    |    |RWF0| 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[R: a1-b1, P: a2-a4, P: a2-a3, P: b2-b4, P: b2-b3, B: c1-d2, B: c1-e3, B: c1-f4, B: c1-g5, B: c1-h6, P: c2-c3, B: c4-b3, B: c4-d3, B: c4-e2, B: c4-f1, B: c4-b5, B: c4-a6, N: d5-b4, N: d5-b6, N: d5-c3, N: d5-c7, N: d5-e3, N: d5-e7, N: d5-f4, N: d5-f6, K: e1-d1, K: e1-d2, K: e1-e2, K: e1-f1, K: e1-g1, P: f2-f4, P: f2-f3, P: g2-g4, P: g2-g3, R: h1-g1, R: h1-f1, P: h2-h4, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': 'Situation: I am playing as White, and the board is in an early-mid game state. My king is still on e1, with both rooks unmoved (a1 and h1), so castling rights are intact. The center is partially open, with a white knight on d5 exerting strong influence. Black’s king is on d7 (KBF1), which is highly unusual and extremely dangerous — it has already moved and is stuck in the center, poorly defended. Black’s pieces are disorganized: their king is exposed, their rooks are on a8 and f8 (but f8 is actually empty — wait, no: RBF1 is on f8? Wait, let\'s recheck.\n\nWait — correction:  \nLooking at rank 8:  \na8: RBF0, b8: NBF0, e8: RBF1 — but no king? Black king is on d7? That can\'t be right in a normal game, but per the board:\n\nBlack king is on d7 (KBF1), which means it has moved and is dangerously exposed. Furthermore, there is no black queen visible — possibly captured or already lost. This suggests Black may have made a blunder or is in a severely compromised position.\n\nMy knight on d5 is attacking key central squares and could be threatening material gain. Additionally, my light-squared bishop on c4 is active, eyeing the weak f7-square (though f7 is not currently weak due to lack of defenders). But more importantly, Black’s king is on d7 — on an open file, surrounded by few defenders, and near the center.\n\nEvaluation:\nLet’s evaluate promising moves:\n\n1. N: d5-b6 — attacks the undefended rook on a8? No: b6 is occupied by nothing, but after Nb6, we threaten the rook on a8 if it\'s undefended. Looking: a8 has RBF0 — yes, rook on a8. Is it defended? c7 has nothing, b7 has PBF0 (b7 pawn), but b6 is empty. So Nb6 would attack a8. But is a8 defended? Possibly by the b7 pawn if it supports... no, pawns don’t defend backward. Likely a8 is only defended by the king on d7 — very far. So Nb6 could win a rook.\n\n2. N: d5-c7+ — this is a check! Knight takes on c7, which is adjacent to d7. c7 currently has no piece (empty), so Nxc7+ is possible. After Nxc7+, Black must respond: King moves or captures. But c7 is not protected by any white piece yet — but the knight gives check. After Nxc7+, Black’s king must move — likely to d8 or e8. Then we can follow up with heavy pressure. Moreover, after capturing on c7, we’re one step from promoting threats or further attacks.\n\n3. B: c4-f7 — bishop to f7? f7 is empty. But f7 is a classic weak spot. However, moving to f7 doesn’t capture anything. But if we could threaten mate… not immediately. And it might hang the bishop.\n\n4. Castling: K: e1-g1 — kingside castling is legal (king hasn\'t moved, rook hasn\'t moved, no pieces between, and king doesn\'t pass through check). This would be prudent in many positions, but here, Black’s king is in the center — they’re not developed or safe. We should punish that first.\n\n5. N: d5-e7 — attacks d5, e7 is currently empty. e7 is adjacent to d7 — so Ne7+ is a check! e7 is not guarded by any black piece? Let\'s see: d6 has PBF1, f6 has nothing, e6 empty. So Ne7+ is also a check! Similar to Nxc7+.\n\nCompare:\n- N: d5-c7+: forks potential with material gain and check.\n- N: d5-e7+: also a check, but e7 is less central than c7? c7 is closer to queenside where black has undeveloped pieces.\n\nBut which square is better?\n\nc7: currently occupied by nothing, and after Nxc7+, Black king must move. If Kd7-d8, then we can play Bf7 or Qa4+ or something later. Also, c7 is behind Black’s queenside — we disrupt their back rank.\n\ne7: similarly, Ne7+ forces Kd7-e8 or Kd7-d6? But d6 has a pawn. Kd7-e8 is forced? Then we have access to d7 later.\n\nBut crucially: **N: d5-c7+** wins material because after Nxc7+, the knight cannot be immediately captured — no black piece defends c7. Then, regardless of where the king goes, we are up a clean pawn (or more) and have a dominant position.\n\nBut wait — is c7 attacked by any black piece?  \n- b6: empty  \n- d6: PBF1 (pawn on d6) — does it attack c7? No, pawns on d6 attack c5 and e5.  \n- b7: PBF0 — attacks c6 only.  \n- e7: empty  \nSo no, c7 is completely undefended. Therefore, **N: d5-c7+** is a free check with a protected knight (no black piece can take it next move unless king does).\n\nAfter Nxc7+, Black must move king:  \n- Kd7-d8  \n- Kd7-e8  \n- Kd7-c8  \n\nAll retreats lose significant material — for example, after Kd7-d8, we can play Rc1, doubling on c-file, or even Nb5, threatening further damage.\n\nAlternatively, **N: d5-b6** doesn\'t give check, and allows Black to play ...Kd7-c6 or develop — worse.\n\nAnother idea: **B: c4-f7+** — bishop check on f7? But f7 is empty — so Bxf7+? Is that possible? f7 has a pawn — PBF0 on f7. So f7 is occupied. Can I take it? Bc4xf7+ — yes! That’s a sacrifice.\n\nBxf7+! — bishop takes on f7, check. King must take back — Kd7xf7. Then we play N: d5-e7+, fork or check. After Kf7, Ne7+ attacks king and potentially wins the rook on f8? e7 checks, king must move — Ke8 or Kf6 or Kg8? But Kxf7, then Ne7+:\n\n- If Kf7-e8, then we have huge initiative, knight on e7, king trapped.\n\nBut is Bxf7+ sound? Let\'s see:\n\n1. Bxf7+ Kxf7  \n2. Ne7+ Ke8 (only)  \nThen we have knight on e7, forking f5 and c6, and threatening Qh5+ or Rf1, etc. Material: we gave up a bishop but have a knight on e7, dominating.\n\nBut compared to **Nxc7+**, which is a free knight fork with check and no risk, why sacrifice?\n\nNxc7+ is safer and still devastating.\n\nMoreover, **N: d5-c7+** is in the valid move list: yes — "N: d5-c7" is listed.\n\nWait — the move is written as **N: d5-c7**, not **N: d5-c7+** — but in our notation, checks aren\'t denoted; only src-dst. So “N: d5-c7” is correct.\n\nNow, is there any danger? Could Black recapture? No — c7 is not defended. So we win a clean pawn and deliver check.\n\nAdditionally, after Nxc7+, Black is in chaos — king exposed, down material, poor development.\n\nAlternative: **N: d5-e7** — also a check? e7 is empty, so Ne7+? Yes — but e7 is not a capture. Still a check. But c7 is deeper into enemy territory and cuts off the king’s escape.\n\nBut Ne7+ doesn’t win material immediately — just gives check. Whereas Nxc7+ wins a pawn *and* gives check.\n\nTherefore, **N: d5-c7** is superior.\n\nAlso, consider: after Nxc7+, if Black plays Kd7-c6, then Nb5 threatens mate or winning the queen — but there is no queen. Still, Nb5+ could be crushing.\n\nBut regardless, Nxc7+ gains material and tempo.\n\nCastling or developing slowly is unnecessary — we must exploit the exposed king immediately.\n\nReason: The black king is stranded on d7, having moved early, and is extremely vulnerable. The move N: d5-c7 delivers a check while capturing an undefended pawn on c7, gaining material and increasing pressure on the exposed king. It is safe, forcing, and maximally punishing Black’s poor king placement.\n\nAnswer: N: d5-c7'}
