 c4.\n\nc4 is a strong post — d3 is more passive.\n\nSo better to push d5-d6.\n\nOr go for the check: Bc4-b5+.\n\nLet’s evaluate B: c4-b5:\n\n- Gives check — forces Black to respond.\n- Black king must move: likely Kd8-e7 or Kd8-c7.\n- Then I can follow up with d5-d6, gaining space and maintaining tension.\n- Also, the bishop on b5 may be hard to dislodge — and could support a future Nb3 or a3, but I have no knights.\n\nStill, it’s a strong centralized bishop.\n\nBut after Kb5+, Black can play Kd8-e7, and then I play d5-d6 — same as before, but I used two moves instead of one.\n\nWhereas if I play d5-d6 first, Black might ignore the threat and play something else, but Rc7 is attacking d5 — so they’ll likely take.\n\nAfter d5-d6, Rc7 is hanging — I can consider Bc4xf7+? But f7 has a knight — NBF1.\n\nBc4xf7+ — is it possible? From c4 to f7 — that’s 3 files right, 3 ranks up — diagonal — yes.\n\nAnd f7 is occupied by enemy knight — so capture is possible.\n\nAnd check? f7 is adjacent to king on d8? f7 to d8 — two files left, one rank up — not adjacent. So not check.\n\nBut after Bxf7+, if king captures, Kd8xf7 — then I have traded bishop for knight, but opened the king.\n\nBut without follow-up, may not be worth it.\n\nBut after Bb5+ first, king moves, then I play d6, I’ve gained two tempi.\n\nBut again, I don’t have time — d5 is under immediate threat.\n\nSo best is to play d5-d6 immediately.\n\nIt saves the pawn, gains space, and keeps the initiative.\n\nCastling is tempting, but losing a pawn for king safety — but the king is not under direct attack, and I can castle next move after d6.\n\nAfter d6, the position is safer.\n\nSo priority is d5-d6.\n\nAnswer: P: d5-d6'}
