king: b1 is empty? Board shows: a1=R, then b1=empty, c1=B, d1=?, e1=K... d1 is empty? Yes. So knight on b1 should be able to go to c3 or a3. But not in valid moves. That implies: perhaps the knight was already moved? Or captured?\n\nLooking at piece encoding: we see RBF0, NBF0 on b8 and a8? No: a8=R, b8=N — both black. White knights: none visible on board? Where are they?\n\nWhite pieces:\n- Pawns: a2,b2,c2,f2,g2,h2 → all present except d2,d6? d6 has white pawn → so d2→d6 via captures?\n- Bishops: c1 and c4 → c1 has BWF0 (never moved), c4 has BWF1 (moved)\n- Rooks: a1 and h1\n- King: e1\n- Queen: missing? Not on board. Must have been captured or not shown.\n\nWait — this is a partial position. We don’t see the queen. But more critically: where are the white knights? They are not on the board. That means they were **captured** or **moved off board**? But they should be on b1 and g1.\n\ng1 is empty, f1 empty, e1=K, d1=empty, etc. So likely, the knight on g1 was captured? But no black pieces nearby.\n\nAlternatively, the valid moves list does **not** include any knight moves — so either they are captured or pinned.\n\nBut the valid moves list includes:\n- P: a2-a4, a2-a3, b2-b4, b2-b3, c2-c3\n- B: c1-d2, e3, f4, g5, h6\n- B: c4-b3, d3, e2, f1, b5, a6, d5, e6, f7\n- P: d6-c7, d6-e7, d6-d7\n- K: e1-d1, d2, e2, f1, g1\n- P: f2-f4, f3; g2-g4, g3; h2-h4, h3\n- R: a1-b1; h1-g1, f1\n\nNo knight moves → therefore, both white knights must have been **captured**. That’s a disaster. But wait — let’s double-check starting setup.\n\nStandard chess: each side has 2 knights. Black shows NBF0 on b8 and NBF1 on g7 → so both black knights are present. White has none visible. Therefore, **both white knights have been captured**. That is extremely bad. We’re down two knights.\n\nBut that seems implausible unless many trades happened. Alternatively, maybe the position is mid-game after heavy exchanges.\n\nBut regardless: we must play the position as given.\n\nSo material count:\n- White: 1