n is near there.\n\nSimilarly:\n- e8: empty\n- f8: NBF1 (Black Knight)\n- g8/h8: empty\n\nOn rank 7:\n- a7: RBF1 (Black Rook)\n- e7: PBF0 (Black Pawn, unmoved)\n\nOn rank 6:\n- a6: PBF1 (Black Pawn, moved)\n\nOn rank 5:\n- b5: PWF1 (White Pawn, already moved)\n- c5: PBF1 (Black Pawn, moved)\n- g5: PBF1 (Black Pawn, moved)\n\nOn rank 4:\n- b4: PWT1 (White Pawn, already moved — likely from b2 to b4 earlier)\n\nThis suggests that b4 has a white pawn that already moved (PWT1), and now on b4.\n\nBut then b5 has PWF1 — that would mean a white pawn on b5? But b5 is occupied by PBF1 per the grid — which says “PBF1” at c5 and “PWF1” at b5.\n\nWait, the grid says:\nRow 5:  \n|    |PWF1|PBF1|    |    |    |PBF1|    |\n\nSo:\n- b5: PWF1 → White pawn\n- c5: PBF1 → Black pawn\n- g5: PBF1 → Black pawn\n\nBut then on row 4:\nb4: PWT1 → another white pawn?\n\nThat’s impossible — two white pawns on b4 and b5? Unless it\'s a promotion or misencoding.\n\nHold on — this seems like a data inconsistency.\n\nLet me parse carefully:\n\nFrom the board:\n\nRow 8: a8: empty, b8: RBF1, c8: KBF1, d8: BWF1 → **White bishop on d8? Impossible unless promoted.**\n\nBut White has no pawns near d8. The only white pawns are:\n- a2: PWF0\n- b2: not shown — but b4 has PWT1 → so a white pawn moved from b2 to b4\n- f2: PWF0\n- g2: PWF0\n- h2: PWF0\n\nAnd on board:\n- b5: PWF1 → white pawn on b5? Then how did it get past b4? b4 also has PWT1 — same pawn?\n\nNo — b4 and b5 both occupied? That would be vertical conflict.\n\nWait — files:\n- b-file:\n  - b8: RBF1\n  - b7: empty\n  - b6: empty\n  - b5: PWF1 → white pawn\n  - b4: PWT1 → white pawn?\n  - b3: empty\n  - b2: ? → not labeled, but likely empty\n\nBut you can\'t have two white pawns on b4 and b5 — same file, adjacent ranks.\n\nUnless... is "PWT1" on b4 a mistake?\n\nLooking back at the board layout:\n\nRow 5: `|    |PWF1|PBF1|    |    |    |PBF1|    |` → so **b5**: PWF1\n\nRow 4: `|    |PWT1|    |    |    |    |    |    |` → so **b4**: PWT1\n\nYes — t