  * `P` - Pawn\n        * Color (Player):\n            * `W` - White (moves first)\n            * `B` - Black\n        * 2Step (En Passant flag)\n            * `F` - Did NOT move 2 squares last turn\n            * `T` - Moved 2 squares last turn (en passant eligible)\n        * Moved (Movement flag)\n            * `1` - Has moved before\n            * `0` - Has NOT moved yet\n        \n        ### Examples: \n            * PWF1: White Pawn, not available for en passant, already moved; \n            * RBF0：Black Rook, never moved (castling eligible);\n            * KWT1: White king, irrelevant 2-step flag, has moved;\n            * QWF0 = White queen, irrelevant 2-step flag, never moved.\n\n        Now is your turn, to your best ability, choose your move to maximize your chances of winning.\n        Please briefly give a overview of the situation, evaluate a few proimsing moves, provide reason for your final choice, and output one of the valid actions with specified format.\n        Your response format should be:\n        Situation: [Your Observation]\n        Evaluation: [Your Evaluation]\n        Reason: [Your Reason]\n        Answer: [Your Answer]\n        ', 'user_prompt': 'You are playing as White.\nCurrent state representation is as follows:\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n8 |    |RBF1|KBF1|NBF1|    |    |    |    | 8\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n7 |RBF1|    |PWF1|    |PBF0|    |    |    | 7\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n6 |PBF1|    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 6\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n5 |    |PWF1|    |    |    |    |    |    | 5\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n4 |    |    |    |    |    |    |PBF1|    | 4\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n3 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 3\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n2 |PWF0|    |    |    |    |PWF0|PWF0|PWF0| 2\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n1 |RWF0|    |    |    |    |RWF1|KWF1|    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[R: a1-b1, R: a1-c1, R: a1-d1, R: a1-e1, P: a2-a4, P: a2-a3, P: b5-a6, P: b5-b6, P: c7-b8-q, P: c7-b8-n, P: c7-b8-b, P: c7-b8-r, P: c7-d8-q, P: c7-d8-n, P: c7-d8-b, P: c7-d8-r, R: f1-e1, R: f1-d1, R: f1-c1, R: f1-b1, P: f2-f4, P: f2-f3, K: g1-h1, P: g2-g3, P: h2-h4, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': 'Situation: I am playing as White, and the board is in a semi-open position with several pawns advanced. My king is on g1 (KWF1), and both rooks are present, with RWF0 on a1 (never moved) and RWF1 on f1. Black has significant material on the kingside and center, including a pawn on e5 (PBF0) that just moved two squares, possibly setting up for en passant vulnerability. My pawn on c7 is one square from promotion, which is a critical tactical opportunity.\n\nThe most pressing opportunity is the pawn on c7, which can promote on the next move to any piece. Promoting immediately gains substantial material and positional advantage. The options are to promote to queen, knight, bishop, or rook — with queen being optimal in most cases due to maximum flexibility and power.\n\nOther moves like a2-a4 or f2-f4 aim to push pawns, but they are less urgent. Castling is not possible because the king has already moved (KWF1). The rook lifts (e.g., Ra1-e1) are passive compared to the immediacy of promotion.\n\nEvaluation:\n- P: c7-b8-q — promotes to queen via capture, gaining material and attacking control.\n- P: c7-d8-q — promotes without capture, slightly less aggressive but still powerful.\n- Other promotions (to knight, bishop, rook) are suboptimal unless for niche tactics — none apply here.\n- Pushing other pawns (a2-a3/a4, f2-f3/f4, h2-h3/h4) are slow and don’t capitalize on the initiative.\n- Rook moves are passive; king move K: g1-h1 is useless.\n\nPromoting the c7 pawn is the highest-value move. Between c7-b8-q and c7-d8-q, capturing on b8 could remove a black rook (RBF1 on b8), but wait — b8 currently has no black piece. Looking back at the board:\n\n- b8 has RBF1? Wait — in rank 8: a8 empty, b8=RBF1? Yes: "RBF1|KBF1|NBF1" — so b8=RBF1, c8=KBF1, d8=NBF1.\n\nWait! That means:\n- b8: RBF1 (Black Rook)\n- c8: KBF1 (Black King)\n- d8: NBF1 (Black Knight)\n\nSo c7 is my pawn (PWF1), on c7, targeting both b8 and d8 for promotion via capture, or straight to d8.\n\nBut c7 is on rank 7, file c. It can move forward to d8 (diagonally forward-left/right only if capturing). But d8 has a black knight (NBF1), and b8 has a black rook (RBF1). So:\n\n- c7 → b8: captures black rook (valuable!)\n- c7 → d8: captures black knight\n\nBoth are captures. But can a pawn promote by moving straight forward?\n\nNo — pawns capture diagonally. To promote to d8, it must be a capture — and d8 is occupied by NBF1, so yes, it\'s capturable.\n\nBut what about moving straight to c8? c8 is occupied by Black King (KBF1), but pawns cannot capture straight ahead — only diagonally.\n\nSo the pawn on c7 can:\n- Capture on b8 (left diagonal): removes black rook\n- Capture on d8 (right diagonal): removes black knight\n\nAnd then promotes.\n\nSince both are captures, we evaluate value:\n- Rook (b8) is more valuable than knight (d8), so capturing rook is better.\n- Also, after capturing rook on b8, promoting to queen gives immediate heavy piece on b8, threatening further damage.\n- However, we must ensure the king isn\'t in check after move.\n\nBlack king is on c8. If I play c7xb8=Q, I remove rook on b8, place queen on b8. Is the black king on c8 attacked? Yes — queen on b8 attacks c8 along the rank. So black king would be in check — good!\n\nMoreover, is the move legal? Yes — pawn captures diagonally forward to b8 (from c7 to b8), captures rook, promotes to queen (or other), and delivers check.\n\nSimilarly, c7xd8=Q would also promote and attack c8, but knight on d8 is less valuable than rook on b8.\n\nThus, c7xb8=Q is superior: higher material gain + check delivered.\n\nNow, looking at valid moves list:\nAvailable: P: c7-b8-q, P: c7-b8-n, etc. — yes, P: c7-b8-q is listed.\n\nTherefore, best move is to capture the rook and promote to queen, delivering check.\n\nReason: Maximizes material gain (rook > knight), promotes to strongest piece, and delivers check, potentially disrupting Black’s coordination. Immediate and decisive.\n\nAnswer: P: c7-b8-q'}
