\n1 |    |    |RWF1|    |    |RWF1|KWF1|    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a2-a4, P: a2-a3, R: c1-b1, R: c1-a1, R: c1-c2, R: c1-c3, R: c1-c4, R: c1-c5, R: c1-c6, R: c1-c7, R: c1-c8, R: c1-d1, R: c1-e1, R: f1-e1, R: f1-d1, R: f1-f2, R: f1-f3, R: f1-f4, R: f1-f5, R: f1-f6, R: f1-f7, K: g1-f2, K: g1-h1, P: g2-g3, P: h2-h4, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I am playing as White, and the board is in the early stages of development. My king is on g1 (KWF1), still in the corner, and both my rooks are on their starting squares (c1 and f1), unmoved. The black knight on f7 and rook on g7 are developed, and there's a black knight threatening central influence. Black has a pawn on g4, slightly advanced but isolated. My pawns on a2, g2, and h2 are unmoved; only a2, g2, and h2 pawns remain on the second rank. Crucially, no pieces have been exchanged yet.\n\nBlack’s knight on f7 could potentially jump to e5 or g5 soon, increasing pressure. My position is undeveloped — only three pawns and two rooks present on the first two ranks. Immediate priorities: develop pieces, control the center, and ensure king safety.\n\nEvaluation:\n- P: a2-a4 – Advances a pawn, gains space on the queenside, but doesn’t contribute to center control or immediate development.\n- P: a2-a3 – Passive, prepares b4 or supports b3 later, but very slow.\n- R: c1-b1 / a1 / d1 / e1 – Moving a rook sideways on the back rank does little without targets or open files. Premature.\n- R: c1-c2 – Develops the rook slightly, supports potential central play, but still passive.\n- R: c1-c3/c4/c5/c6/c7/c8 – Advancing the rook up the c-file might be useful later, but currently lacks purpose with no pressure on that file.\n- R: f1-e1/d1/f2/f3/f4/f5/f6/f7 – Similarly, moving f1 rook forward prematurely risks exposure. However, R: f1-f3 or f4 could support central control later.\n- K: g1-f2 or h1 – King moves early are generally bad unless forced; castling is better for king safety.\n- P: g2-g3 – This move prepares to develop the bishop to g2 via fianchetto, a solid developing plan. It also connects with potential central control (supporting d4 later) and keeps options open.\n- P: h2-h3 – Very passive, stops ...Ng4 ideas but Black isn't threatening that immediately.\n- P: h2-h4 – Ambitious, aims for kingside expansion, but weakens the g1-a7 diagonal and may leave h4-pawn exposed.\n\nBest developing moves are P: g2-g3 and possibly P: a2-a4 for space. But g2-g3 stands out because it enables rapid development of the dark-squared bishop to g2, where it will control the long diagonal (a8-h1), support central play, and prepare for kingside castling.\n\nReason: P: g2-g3 is a flexible, purposeful developing move that enables quick completion of development (Bishop to g2, then castle). It avoids passivity while not overextending. Compared to h2-h3/h4 or a2-a3/a4, it contributes more directly to king safety and central influence.\n\nAnswer: P: g2-g3"}
