\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |RWF1|KWF1|    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a4-a5, R: b4-b3, R: b4-b2, R: b4-b1, R: b4-b5, R: b4-b6, R: b4-b7, R: b4-b8, R: b4-c4, R: b4-d4, R: b4-e4, R: b4-f4, R: b4-g4, R: f1-e1, R: f1-d1, R: f1-c1, R: f1-b1, R: f1-a1, R: f1-f2, R: f1-f3, R: f1-f4, R: f1-f5, R: f1-f6, R: f1-f7, R: f1-f8, K: g1-f2, K: g1-g2, K: g1-h1, P: h2-h4, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I am playing as White, and the board is highly asymmetric. My king is on g1 (KWF1), with a rook on f1 (RWF1) that has already moved, so castling long is not possible. There is another rook on b4 (RWF1), which is active and central. The Black king is on d5 (KBF1), surprisingly exposed in the center, with minimal nearby support. Black has a knight on h6 (NBF1) and a rook on g8 (RBF1), both undeveloped or passively placed. Black’s pawns include one on g7 (PBF1) and one on g4 (PWF1 — wait, this must be a typo in labeling; likely PBF1). My pawn on h2 (PWF0) hasn't moved yet.\n\nBlack's king on d5 is extremely vulnerable — it's deep in the center, far from safety, and lacks defenders. This is a critical weakness. My pieces are reasonably placed to exploit this: the rook on f1 can potentially target the king along the first rank if files open, and the rook on b4 can become active quickly. Most importantly, advancing the h-pawn could open the h-file for attack, especially since Black’s king is trapped in the center.\n\nEvaluation:\n- P: h2-h4 — Opens the possibility of rapid kingside attack, prepares to lift the rook via h3 or g3, and activates the h-file. Since h2-h4 uses the two-step option, it also makes en passant impossible later unless needed, but more importantly, it accelerates attacking chances.\n- R: b4-b8 — Threatens immediate capture on b8 if Black doesn’t respond, but Black can simply play ...Rxb8, trading rooks and relieving pressure. Premature.\n- R: f1-f5/f6/f7 — Aggressive, but currently not supported enough. Risky without backup.\n- K: g1-g2/h1/f2 — King movement is unnecessary and wastes tempo when opponent’s king is more exposed.\n- P: a4-a5 — Passive, sidelines the pawn, no immediate threat.\n\nThe most promising idea is to launch a quick attack on the kingside where the Black king is stranded. Playing h2-h4 opens lines and threatens h4-h5, followed by Rh3-g3 or similar, targeting the weak g7 and h7 squares. Even though Black may try to run (...Ke4, etc.), their position is nearly lost if we act swiftly.\n\nReason: The key principle here is **exploiting the exposed king**. Black’s monarch on d5 is in grave danger. While they may try to escape, every move we make should increase pressure. Advancing h2-h4 is a dynamic, non-committal yet aggressive move that opens the h-file and enables rapid mobilization of rooks or queen (if developed). It gains time and space.\n\nAnswer: P: h2-h4"}
