, R: b4-b7 reaches deep into Black’s side and threatens invasion.\n- King moves like K: g1-h1 or K: g1-f2 are quiet, but there's no immediate danger, so wasting tempo may not be ideal.\n- R: f1-f4 or R: f1-f5 prepares to double rooks or attack on the f-file, but less urgent.\n- P: h4-h5 gains space and prevents ...g7-g6 followed by Nh6 or Ng4.\n\nBut most promising:\n- R: b4-b8+ would be a strong check if Black’s king were near, but b8 is currently just a positional threat, possibly preparing for future tactics.\n- However, more immediately useful is R: b4-b7, placing rook on 7th rank (Black’s second rank), which often leads to winning pawns or restricting the king.\n- But wait — Black has no pawns on the 7th rank, so this is more about control than immediate gain.\n- Still, R: b4-b7 is very strong, as it cuts off the Black king’s escape and threatens to trap the knight or rook.\n- Alternatively, R: b4-g4 targets the weak g7 square and eyes the h-file where h4-h5 is coming.\n\nBut note: Black’s knight on f5 is unprotected and on f5 — which is attacked by my pawn on g4? No, I have a pawn on h4, not g4. So f5 knight is not under direct attack.\n\nWait — I have a pawn on h4 and one on a4. The knight on f5 is supported by nothing? Let's see — no, Black king is on d5, so knight on f5 is likely protected via ...e.g., e7 or something, but we don’t see full board history.\n\nStill, no immediate tactical shot.\n\nBest idea: increase pressure. R: b4-b7 is strong — places rook on 7th rank, threatens to cut off Black king, and supports future action.\n\nAlternatively, R: f1-f5 could put pressure on the f5 knight. That might be better!\n\nR: f1-f5 — attacks the knight on f5 directly! And the knight is on f5, and I have no other piece attacking it — but if I play R: f1-f5, then I attack it.\n\nIs the knight defended?\n\nBlack king is on d5 — that's two squares away; it does not defend f5. Is there any other defender? We don't see any other pieces — so unless there's a hidden bish