  * `P` - Pawn\n        * Color (Player):\n            * `W` - White (moves first)\n            * `B` - Black\n        * 2Step (En Passant flag)\n            * `F` - Did NOT move 2 squares last turn\n            * `T` - Moved 2 squares last turn (en passant eligible)\n        * Moved (Movement flag)\n            * `1` - Has moved before\n            * `0` - Has NOT moved yet\n        \n        ### Examples: \n            * PWF1: White Pawn, not available for en passant, already moved; \n            * RBF0：Black Rook, never moved (castling eligible);\n            * KWT1: White king, irrelevant 2-step flag, has moved;\n            * QWF0 = White queen, irrelevant 2-step flag, never moved.\n\n        Now is your turn, to your best ability, choose your move to maximize your chances of winning.\n        Please briefly give a overview of the situation, evaluate a few proimsing moves, provide reason for your final choice, and output one of the valid actions with specified format.\n        Your response format should be:\n        Situation: [Your Observation]\n        Evaluation: [Your Evaluation]\n        Reason: [Your Reason]\n        Answer: [Your Answer]\n        ', 'user_prompt': 'You are playing as White.\nCurrent state representation is as follows:\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n8 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 8\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n7 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 7\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n6 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 6\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n5 |    |    |    |KBF1|    |    |    |    | 5\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n4 |PWF1|RWF1|    |    |    |    |PBF1|    | 4\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n3 |    |    |    |    |    |    |PWF1|    | 3\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n2 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 2\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |KWF1|    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a4-a5, R: b4-b3, R: b4-b2, R: b4-b1, R: b4-b5, R: b4-b6, R: b4-b7, R: b4-b8, R: b4-c4, R: b4-d4, R: b4-e4, R: b4-f4, R: b4-g4, K: g1-f1, K: g1-f2, K: g1-g2, K: g1-h1, K: g1-h2]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': 'Situation: I\'m playing as White, and the board is nearly empty. The only pieces left are the two kings, one white pawn on a4, one white rook on b4, and one black pawn on g4. My king is on g1, and the black king is on d5, which is deep in the center. The black king is quite active, while my pieces are somewhat passive. The white pawn on a4 could potentially advance to promote, but it\'s far from the promotion square. The rook on b4 has a lot of mobility along the 4th rank and the b-file.\n\nEvaluation:\n- Moving the a-pawn (a4-a5) would gain space on the queenside but doesn\'t contribute much immediately; it also risks allowing the black king to capture it if not supported.\n- The rook has many options. Moving it up the b-file (b4-b8) might be too passive or even risky without targets.\n- Advancing the rook along the 4th rank (e.g., b4-e4) could control central squares and restrict the black king’s movement.\n- Bringing the king forward (g1-f2, g1-g2, etc.) activates the king, which is crucial in endgames. Centralizing the king is often key in king and pawn endgames.\n- The move K: g1-f2 brings the king closer to the center and supports potential action on the kingside, possibly supporting the g3 pawn or contesting central files.\n\nHowever, the most critical issue is that Black’s king is centralized and active, while mine is stuck in the corner. I need to activate my king immediately. The best way to improve my position is to start moving the king toward the center.\n\nAmong the king moves:\n- K: g1-f1 – moves inward but stays passive.\n- K: g1-f2 – good: heads toward e3/d4, more central.\n- K: g1-g2 – decent, prepares f3 or h3, but less central.\n- K: g1-h1 or h2 – heading to the edge, worse.\n\nK: g1-f2 is the most logical developing move for the king.\n\nBut wait — there\'s a white pawn on g3 (implied by the black pawn on g4 capturing something? No — actually, looking again: the board shows PBF1 on g4 — that\'s a black pawn on g4. And PWF1 on g3? Wait — no, there\'s no piece listed on g3. On g3, there is nothing marked — but on g4 there is PBF1 (Black Pawn, moved, en passant eligible? But it didn\'t just move two squares — its flag is F, so not en passant eligible). Actually, the only pawns are:\n- PWF1 on a4 → white pawn, already moved\n- PBF1 on g4 → black pawn, already moved\n- PWF1 on g3 → wait, no! Looking at rank 3: "PWF1" is on g3 — yes! In the row for rank 3: "| | | | | | |PWF1| |" → that\'s g3.\n\nSo:\n- White pawn on a4 (PWF1)\n- White pawn on g3 (PWF1)\n- Black pawn on g4 (PBF1)\n\nAh! There\'s a direct opposition: black pawn on g4, white pawn on g3. So they are adjacent.\n\nWait — g3: PWF1 (white pawn), g4: PBF1 (black pawn). So they are on adjacent squares, same file? No — same file g, ranks 3 and 4. So they are on g3 and g4 — same file, adjacent ranks. That means they are blocking each other — neither can advance unless one captures, but no legal capture exists since pawns capture diagonally.\n\nSo both pawns are currently blocked.\n\nTherefore, the g-pawn chain is locked.\n\nNow, back to strategy.\n\nThe black king is on d5 — very central. It can head toward the queenside to eat the a4 pawn or support its own g4 pawn.\n\nMy king is on g1 — far away. I must bring it into play.\n\nThe rook on b4 can be useful to control open lines or support passed pawns, but currently, there are no immediate tactical threats.\n\nBest plan: Activate the king. Move Kg1-f2 is a step toward centralization.\n\nAlternatively, consider rook activity. Rook to e4 (R: b4-e4) controls the 4th rank, potentially restricting the black king’s access to e5, e6, etc., and supports the a4 pawn indirectly by controlling the rank.\n\nBut the rook on b4-e4 is a long move, but possible.\n\nCompare:\n- R: b4-e4 — controls central squares, may prevent black king from advancing to e5 or d4 via the 4th rank.\n- K: g1-f2 — starts king activation.\n\nSince the black king is already on d5, it can easily walk to c4 or c5 and threaten the a4 pawn. I need either to support a4 with the rook or king, or push it.\n\nBut a4-a5 would create a target unless supported.\n\nIf I play a4-a5, then after ...Kc5, the pawn is under pressure.\n\nBetter to prepare support.\n\nRook on b4-e4 eyes the e-file and the 4th rank. But e4 is not directly protecting a4.\n\nRook on b4-d4 would be better to protect the a4 pawn along the 4th rank.\n\nIs b4-d4 a valid move? Looking at the list: R: b4-b3, b4-b2, ..., R: b4-c4, R: b4-d4 — yes! R: b4-d4 is in the list.\n\nR: b4-d4 places the rook on the same rank as the a4 pawn, thus defending it. Then I can consider pushing a4-a5 with support.\n\nThat seems more proactive than just moving the king now.\n\nAlternatively, combine plans: first defend the pawn, then advance.\n\nCurrently, if Black plays ...Kd5-c5, next ...Kb4, it attacks a4. With rook on b4, if I don’t defend, Black can win the pawn.\n\nSo I should defend the a4 pawn before Black’s king arrives.\n\nThus, R: b4-d4 is excellent — defends a4 via the 4th rank.\n\nAfter R: b4-d4, if Black takes ...Kc5, I can play a4-a5, creating a protected passed pawn.\n\nEven better.\n\nKing movement is important, but losing the a-pawn gives Black a winning advantage probably, as their king can then shepherd their g4 pawn.\n\nBut with two pawns vs one, and active king, it\'s complex.\n\nBut preserving the a-pawn is key.\n\nSo rook move to d4 is strong.\n\nOther rook moves like b4-c4 also defend a4, but d4 is more central.\n\nb4-d4 is listed, so it\'s allowed.\n\nAlso, rook to f4, g4? g4 is occupied by black pawn, so cannot go there.\n\nb4-g4 is in the list — but g4 has a black pawn, so unless it\'s a capture, it\'s invalid.\n\nWait — the move list includes R: b4-g4 — but g4 is occupied by an enemy piece, so it should be a capture.\n\nBut in the representation, can the rook capture on g4?\n\nYes — rook moves horizontally — from b4 to g4 is along rank 4, and g4 has a black pawn, so R: b4-g4 would be a capture.\n\nThat’s a possibility!\n\nR: b4-g4 captures the black pawn on g4!\n\nIs that legal? Yes — rook moves along the 4th rank from b4 to g4, passing over c4, d4, e4, f4 — all empty — so yes, it’s a legal move.\n\nAnd it captures the black pawn on g4.\n\nThat removes a potential threat — the black pawn was on g4, which could have advanced to g3 and promoted, but it was blocked by the white pawn on g3.\n\nWait — white pawn on g3, black on g4 — so black pawn is on g4, cannot advance, and white pawn on g3 cannot advance because black pawn is directly above.\n\nSo both are blocked — frozen.\n\nBut if I capture on g4 with the rook, then the black pawn is gone, and now I have a rook on g4.\n\nThen, the white pawn on g3 is still there, but now it can advance: g3-g4, then g4-g5, etc., potentially promoting.\n\nMoreover, removing the black pawn eliminates a potential passer once freed.\n\nAdditionally, it activates my rook to the kingside.\n\nThis seems very strong.\n\nWhy would I not do this?\n\nLet’s see: after R: b4-g4 (capturing the black pawn), the position becomes:\n\n- White rook on g4\n- White pawns on a4 and g3\n- Black king on d5\n- Black has no pawns\n\nI can follow up with g3-g4, advancing my own pawn.\n\nMeanwhile, Black may try to take the a4 pawn, but I can defend it or race.\n\nBut now I have a 