ack king is on b6, placing the rook on b7 delivers check and restricts escape to a7, c7, a6, c6, etc. This seems powerful.\n- R: g7-a7 – Same file, but too far and less effective than b7.\n- R: g7-c7 – Also cuts off, but not with check; b7 is better because it attacks b6 directly.\n- K: g1-f1/f2/g2/h1/h2 – King moves are slow and unnecessary now; no urgency.\n- P: a4-a5 – Pushes pawn, but doesn’t contribute to attack.\n- P: g3-g4 – Pushes pawn, but again, not impactful.\n\nBest move appears to be **R: g7-b7**, which checks the Black king and severely limits its mobility.\n\nReason: Delivering check with the rook to b7 forces the Black king to respond immediately. It cannot capture the rook (no piece can), and likely must move to a6, c6, or a5 (if available), all of which may lead to faster zugzwang or mating patterns. This maximizes pressure and minimizes Black’s counterplay.\n\nAnswer: R: g7-b7"}
