\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |KWF1|    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a4-a5, K: g1-f1, K: g1-f2, K: g1-g2, K: g1-h1, K: g1-h2, P: g3-g4]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: [The board is nearly empty, with only three pawns and two kings remaining. White has pawns on a4 and g3, while Black has a lone pawn on b7. Both kings are active but not in immediate danger. There are no pieces left to deliver checkmate except for the pawns potentially promoting. The key to winning is promoting a pawn before the opponent does. Currently, White to move.]\n\nEvaluation: [Two main plans exist: advancing the a-pawn or the g-pawn. \n- Pushing a4-a5 slightly improves the a-pawn's march but doesn't accelerate it dramatically.\n- Advancing g3-g4 supports potential future central expansion (though irrelevant here), but more importantly, it activates the kingside pawn chain.\n- The king moves (g1-f1, f2, g2, h1, h2) aim to centralize. Since endgames with few pawns often require king activity to support promotion or block enemy pawns, moving the king closer to the center or toward a pawn is critical.\n- The black pawn on b7 is one step away from queening if unopposed. However, it’s on b7, and Black’s king is absent — but wait: Black *does* have a king on b7? Wait — no! The piece on b7 is labeled KBF1 — that’s the **Black King** on b7! So Black's king is dangerously close to White's a4 pawn.\n\nThis changes everything: the black king is on b7, ready to march toward the a-pawn and capture it unless opposed. Meanwhile, White's king is on g1, far away. Immediate action is needed to prevent loss.\n\nSo current priorities:\n- If White plays passively, Black can play ...Kb7-a6, then capture the a4 pawn. Then Black may promote their own pawn? Wait — there is no black pawn at all! Only the black king on b7, white pawns on a4 and g3. So Black has no pawns. That means the only way Black can win is by capturing both pawns and forcing a draw. But if White promotes even one pawn, they can win.\n\nTherefore, White must avoid stalemate and aim to promote a pawn. The best chance is to push one of the pawns with king support.\n\nBut currently, if White doesn’t move the king toward the pawns, especially the a-pawn, Black will simply walk over and capture it. So White must start supporting the a-pawn’s advance.\n\nThus, moving the king toward the queenside (a-file) is essential. However, the only available king moves are toward the kingside (f1, f2, g2, h1, h2) — all in the wrong direction!\n\nWait — this seems odd. Is the king really restricted to only those directions?\n\nLooking again: the king is on g1. Legal moves are:\n- f1 (left), f2 (diagonally up-left), g2 (up), h1 (right), h2 (diagonally up-right)\n\nYes — none of these go toward the a-file. All are right or center. So we cannot immediately head toward the a-pawn.\n\nBut we must eventually. However, we need to make progress now.\n\nBetween the two pawns, the a4 pawn is more advanced and closer to promotion (only 4 moves to a8). But it's vulnerable. The black king on b7 can reach a5 in two moves: Kb7-b6-a6, then Ka6xa5.\n\nSo White must either:\n1. Advance the pawn quickly before Black captures it.\n2. Bring the king across the board to defend/support.\n\nBut the king is very far.\n\nLet’s analyze the race:\n- Black king on b7 → can reach a5 in 2 moves (b7-a6-a5)\n- White pawn on a4 → needs 4 moves to promote: a4-a5-a6-a7-a8\n- After a4-a5, Black can play Ka6, then if a5-a6, Ka5 captures on a6? No — Ka6-a5 attacks a5 square.\n\nSo after a4-a5, Black plays Kb7-a6,