next move Ka5xa6.\n\nBut White can play a5-a6 only if supported.\n\nBut currently unsupported.\n\nSo better to play a4-a5, then use the next few moves to bring the king.\n\nBut Black might just take on a5 immediately after a4-a5? No — after a4-a5, the pawn is on a5, Black king on b7 — distance is two squares (b7-c6-a5 or b7-a6-a5). So needs two moves to reach a5.\n\nSo White has two free moves after a4-a5.\n\nIn those two moves, White can play, say, g1-f2 and then f2-e3, starting the journey.\n\nWhereas if White doesn’t push the pawn, Black may play Kb7-a6 immediately, then next move Ka6-a5, attacking a4.\n\nSo pushing a4-a5 first gains a tempo — now Black must spend two moves to attack it, same as before, but White has advanced.\n\nMoreover, after a4-a5, the pawn is one step closer to promotion.\n\nSo a4-a5 is better than waiting.\n\nNow compare to g3-g4: it does nothing useful. The g-pawn is irrelevant unless the a-pawn is lost.\n\nKing moves like g1-f2 are decent but passive — they don’t create a threat.\n\nBy contrast, a4-a5 creates a threat and accelerates the win condition.\n\nTherefore, best move: P: a4-a5\n\nReason: [Pushing the a-pawn immediately begins its promotion journey while forcing Black to respond. Although the pawn will become vulnerable, it gains a crucial tempo. Without this push, Black can advance their king for free. This move maximizes winning chances by creating an immediate threat and utilizing the most advanced pawn. The king will follow later.]\n\nAnswer: P: a4-a5"}
